            "doc": "Segment tree beats: range chmin (a[i] = min(a[i], x)), range add and range assign, with range sums and the range max / min, plus point ops. All indices 0-based, ranges inclusive. How it works: a chmin only changes the max values of a node it covers, so a node keeps its max, its strict second max and how many copies of the max it has; a chmin x with mx2 < x < mx moves just those copies (the sum drops by (mx - x) * cntMx) and stops there, and only x <= mx2 has to go further down. A node's own mx doubles as its pending clamp: push clamps each child to it, so there is no chmin / chmax tag. There is no assign tag either: when all of a node's values are equal (no second max), push hands that value to both children as an assign. An add shifts everything a node keeps and goes in the one add tag. Amortized O(log^2 N) per update with range add, O(log N) without it (beats: every extra descent removes a distinct value).",
