            "doc": "An array whose ranges you can sort in place (ascending or descending), amortized O(log N + log U) a sort, with point reads / writes, rangeAssign, range sums, range xors, range maxes, insert / erase / reverse / rotate / move anywhere and the whole array. It is kept as a sequence of sorted runs: each run is a dynamic value seg tree over [valLo, valHi] (copies per node) and an up / down flag, and the runs, in order, are the nodes of an implicit treap (random priorities, expected depth log N), each also keeping its subtree's length and sum / xor / max; a reverse flips the order of the runs and each run's up / down flag. Sorting [l, r] cuts the runs at l and r + 1 and merges every run inside into one; a[i] is the k-th value of the run holding it; a query cuts at l and r + 1 too, then reads whole runs. Merges cost about as many nodes as they free and every run is merged away at most once, hence the amortized bounds (the treap's are expected as well). Indices 0-based, ranges inclusive, every index is a position in the current array. T: int, long long, ...; sums in S (long long for small T).",
